{"vid":"xk5rp4qCU8M","source":"xk5rp4qCU8M.en-en.json3","lang":"en","cues":[{"t":1.1,"txt":"Business Skills 360, the podcast that looks at the other side of English."},{"t":11.2,"txt":"Hello and welcome back to the Skills 360 podcast. I'm your host, Tim Simmons, and today I want"},{"t":20.4,"txt":"to continue our look at tips for succeeding in a job interview in English."},{"t":28.1,"txt":"In our last lesson, we talked about preparation for introducing yourself and questions about"},{"t":36.3,"txt":"strengths and weaknesses. That's all about you as a person, or your character. In this"},{"t":45.8,"txt":"lesson, I'd like to home in on what you've done, or your actions and behavior."},{"t":53.7,"txt":"The first big question you'll get about what you have done pertains to achievements. As"},{"t":61.9,"txt":"in: \"what achievements are you most proud of?\" Or \"tell us about a recent achievement?\""},{"t":70.1,"txt":"Now, when you think back on your accomplishments, what should you choose to discuss? Well, rather"},{"t":79.4,"txt":"than boasting about purely individual accomplishments, think of something that connects to the bigger"},{"t":86.5,"txt":"picture. Or state why your accomplishment helped the company."},{"t":91.9,"txt":"So, don't just say \"I oversaw an expansion of our department from four to nine people.\""},{"t":101.6,"txt":"Link that to overall company goals, or say why that is an achievement. So you could add"},{"t":110.3,"txt":"something like: \"That helped support the company in its overall growth goals.�"},{"t":117.8,"txt":"Another important point when talking about accomplishments: give credit where credit"},{"t":124,"txt":"is due, and demonstrate gratitude for others'"},{"t":129.3,"txt":"efforts. Yes, it's a question about you."},{"t":134,"txt":"But most positions require teamwork, and so most companies are looking for team players."},{"t":140.7,"txt":"That's why it's a good idea to throw in something like \"of course, none of it would"},{"t":148.4,"txt":"have been possible without a stellar team of salespeople.\""},{"t":153.8,"txt":"Besides questions about your achievements, you're likely to face questions about past"},{"t":159.7,"txt":"behavior. Especially common is the question \"tell us about a time when you demonstrated"},{"t":167,"txt":"a particular quality?\" For example, leadership, or problem-solving, or creativity. In this"},{"t":178.8,"txt":"case, the interviewers want some evidence, or proof, of your abilities."},{"t":186.5,"txt":"It's a good idea to have some situations in mind before your interview. And you don't"},{"t":195.2,"txt":"have to think up different situations for every imaginable quality or characteristic."},{"t":202.2,"txt":"Instead, think of a few situations that you could use for different qualities. I mean,"},{"t":211.8,"txt":"maybe there's a particular time when your leadership, problem-solving, and creativity"},{"t":218.9,"txt":"all came into play. You can bring that situation up in response to a variety of questions."},{"t":228,"txt":"And when you discuss what you did, keep it to three simple parts: the situation, what"},{"t":236.5,"txt":"you did, and the result. Here's an example of this approach to a question about problem-solving:"},{"t":245.2,"txt":"\"Well, just last month we had a big project with a demanding client, and three developers"},{"t":253.7,"txt":"got sick. There was no way we could finish on time without help. So I managed to flex"},{"t":260.7,"txt":"some other projects and transfer some staff. In the end, we had good outcomes and happy"},{"t":268.1,"txt":"clients.\""},{"t":270.9,"txt":"That was a response to a question about problem-solving. But notice that I could easily modify the"},{"t":278.4,"txt":"answer to fit a question about leadership, or handling clients, or project management."},{"t":286.6,"txt":"That's what I mean about keeping a few versatile situations in mind, especially if you're"},{"t":293.9,"txt":"not so confident in your interview English skills."},{"t":299.2,"txt":"Okay, so there's one more issue I want to address: questions about conflict. As in \"tell"},{"t":309.5,"txt":"us about a time you had to deal with conflict with a co-worker?\" Or \"tell me about a"},{"t":316.4,"txt":"time you had a major disagreement with your boss?\""},{"t":321.5,"txt":"With questions about conflict, interviewers want to see that you can communicate, that"},{"t":328.3,"txt":"you care about relationships, and - ultimately - that you're not a jerk. For that last"},{"t":336.6,"txt":"reason, the worst thing you can do is to throw someone under the bus or even just to sound"},{"t":344.2,"txt":"bitter. So follow the same formula of: situation, what you did, and the result. And spin the"},{"t":354.7,"txt":"conflict as a constructive experience."},{"t":359.4,"txt":"For example, you might say something like: \"well, a co-worker was talking about me"},{"t":365.5,"txt":"behind my back. After a few tough weeks, I took her for coffee, and I asked her point"},{"t":372.1,"txt":"blank what was going on. Turns out she felt threatened by my success. I managed to allay"},{"t":379.7,"txt":"her fears, and now we get along great.\" Does that make sense? Situation, action, and"},{"t":389,"txt":"result, with a positive and constructive focus."},{"t":395.3,"txt":"All right, so today we've focused on English interview tips for answering questions about"},{"t":402.6,"txt":"your experience and behavior. That includes accomplishments, which should be connected"},{"t":409.8,"txt":"to the big picture and shown to include others. We've also talked about how to answer behavioral"},{"t":417.8,"txt":"questions using a situation, action, result response. And remember to think about some"},{"t":427,"txt":"positive and negative situations beforehand, so you're ready."},{"t":434,"txt":"That's all for today."},{"t":436.3,"txt":"So long. And see you again soon."},{"t":437.3,"txt":"www.BusinessEnglishPod.com"},{"t":438.3,"txt":"Skills 360 - English Interview Tips 2"}